Ladybird validates every locale catalog as the extension loads,
and merges the user’s first preferred language, that language’s
shorter tags, and default_locale, the earliest winning for
each message. Chromium also validates catalogs no fallback
uses, in ValidateExtensionLocales(), and builds its chain in
GetAllFallbackLocales() from the application locale’s lineage.
Gecko’s ExtensionData.initLocale() loads the preferred locale’s
variations and the default, and WebKit’s WebExtensionLocalization::
loadRegionalLocalization() merges regional, language-only, and
default catalogs. Ladybird has no separate application language, so its
Accept-Language list stands in for one.

Ladybird localizes only name, short_name, description, and the
action titles of the manifest, a field list like the one in Chromium’s
LocalizeManifest(). Gecko localizes the fields its schemas mark as
localizable, and WebKit’s localizedJSONforJSON() walks every string in
the manifest. All three engines make the extension id and the UI locale
and direction messages available without any catalog, which is what lets
content-script CSS name the extension’s own URLs. Ladybird localizes
declared content-script CSS before it goes to WebContent, as WebKit’s
WebExtensionContext::localizedResourceString() does for injected CSS.
